\section{Empirical Results: 0 bps Transaction Costs}

This section presents the core results in a frictionless setting (0 bps proportional transaction costs), emphasizing how outcomes vary by risk proxy and rebalancing schedule at a fixed, pre-specified annualized risk budget. The paper presents the main figures and summary table in-text; additional diagnostic tables/figures across the full risk-budget grid are generated by the build pipeline but are not all reproduced in the main PDF to keep the paper compact.

Because the backtest evaluates a grid of specifications (risk budgets, frequencies, and risk proxies), the main-text table below fixes the risk budget at 10\% annualized across all strategies and frequencies, rather than reporting the budget that maximizes terminal wealth for each cell. This choice keeps the primary comparison free of ex-post selection over the risk-budget dimension, consistent with the paper's broader design discipline of a small, fixed, fully crossed grid rather than a search over configurations (Section 2.3/3.7). Results at the other risk budgets (5\% and 15\%) are reported in full in the supplementary build output and referenced in-text where they materially change the interpretation.

\paragraph{What to look for in a frictionless setting.} Before introducing transaction costs, it is useful to separate two questions: (i) whether rebalancing frequency changes performance mechanically through estimation-window length and how often the optimizer is ``allowed'' to react, and (ii) whether swapping the dispersion proxy inside the risk constraint materially changes outcomes once implementability constraints (long-only, max-weight cap, and an explicit cash sleeve) are imposed. The results below provide direct evidence on both dimensions and motivate why turnover will become the dominant channel once proportional costs are introduced.

