\subsection{Drawdowns and Downside Risk}

Two drawdown measures are used throughout this paper: a within-period maximum drawdown (MDD), computed from the peak-to-trough decline within each holding period and then summarized as the worst such value over time, and a full-sample MDD, computed from the peak-to-trough decline of the entire cumulative wealth path across the whole backtest, independent of rebalance boundaries (Section~3.8.3). \textbf{The discussion, Table~\ref{tab:results_10pct}, and Figure 5 below must be updated to report both measures, with the full-sample MDD as the primary cross-strategy comparison figure}, since the original draft of this section only discussed the within-period measure.

\emph{[Figure 5. Full-sample and within-period maximum drawdown heatmaps at 0 bps transaction costs. Each panel shows one strategy; axes represent risk budgets (vertical) and rebalancing schedules (horizontal). Cell values indicate maximum drawdown magnitude in percent (higher values indicate larger drawdowns).]}

\paragraph{Relative performance and patterns.} [To be rewritten once both MDD measures are available across the fixed-budget grid. The original draft claimed that (i) drawdowns are driven primarily by market regime and effective risk exposure, (ii) worst within-period drawdowns tend to be materially larger at quarterly-to-annual horizons than at the monthly horizon, and (iii) drawdown differences across dispersion proxies are generally modest relative to the rebalance-interval effect. Point (ii) is partly mechanical for the within-period measure, since longer holding periods can realize deeper peak-to-trough moves before the next rebalance resets exposure; this mechanical component should be explicitly separated from any genuine risk-proxy effect once the full-sample MDD series is added, since the full-sample measure does not reset at rebalance boundaries and should be less mechanically sensitive to holding-period length.]

\subsubsection*{A note on near-identical MDD values across proxies at a fixed frequency}
\label{sec:mdd-check}
In the original best-of-three draft, several rebalancing frequencies showed MDD values that were identical (to two decimal places) across all four risk proxies within that frequency (e.g., all four strategies at $-36.68$ for 3-month rebalancing, all four at $-33.55$ for 6-month rebalancing). \textbf{This should be verified against the underlying code before the fixed-budget table is finalized.} A plausible, non-error explanation is that all four strategies were simultaneously fully de-risked into the cash sleeve or bound by the maximum-weight cap during the same market stress episode (producing an identical realized path for that sub-period); however, given how unusual bit-for-bit identical drawdowns are across independently constrained optimizations, this pattern should be positively confirmed rather than assumed benign, since it is also consistent with an implementation error (e.g., a single MDD column being inadvertently reused across strategy rows).

\subsection{Realized Risk and Risk-Adjusted Performance}

To keep the main text streamlined, this subsection focuses on realized annualized volatility and the return-to-volatility ratio (CAGR/AnnVol, with $r_f = 0$), both reported alongside performance in Table~\ref{tab:results_10pct}. Alternative proxy-specific return-to-risk ratios and related diagnostic grids are reported in the supplementary build output.

\paragraph{Relative performance and patterns.} [To be reconfirmed at the fixed 10\% budget:] realized dispersion is expected to increase with the stated risk budget across all strategies, indicating that the annual-to-daily scaling and proxy-to-proxy calibration (Section~3.6) produce broadly sensible monotonic behavior in realized risk; this should be checked across the 5\%/10\%/15\% grid in the supplementary output. Within a fixed (risk, frequency) cell, realized risk levels are expected to be broadly similar across the four optimized strategies, given that all optimizers face the same implementation constraints and target dispersion budgets on the same underlying daily-return data --- though see the caveat below.

\paragraph{A caveat on IQR's terminal-wealth advantage.}
\label{sec:iqr-caveat}
In the original best-of-three draft, the IQR-constrained strategy showed both the highest terminal wealth in most cells \emph{and} noticeably higher realized annualized volatility than the other three proxies within the same nominal risk budget (e.g., 11.43 vs.\ 10.91--11.35 at 1mo; 14.62 vs.\ 13.61--14.12 at 6mo, all at the 15\% nominal target). This raises the possibility that IQR's apparent outperformance reflects the normal-approximation calibration (Section~3.6, Equation~17) producing a systematically looser \emph{realized} budget for IQR relative to the other proxies under fat-tailed returns, rather than a genuine economic advantage of the IQR-based constraint. \textbf{Before claiming that ``proxy choice is second-order,'' this section should explicitly report realized volatility alongside terminal wealth for each proxy and, ideally, show results conditioned on comparable realized (not just nominal target) risk-taking across proxies.} If IQR's terminal-wealth edge shrinks or disappears once realized volatility is held approximately equal across proxies, that materially changes the ``second-order'' framing used throughout this section and the paper's abstract.

In terms of risk-adjusted performance, [to be reconfirmed once the fixed-budget table and the realized-volatility check above are complete:] differences across proxies are present but not uniform across rebalance intervals. This reinforces the paper's central theme: in a constrained, implementable setting, the interaction between estimation window length and rebalance frequency can dominate the incremental choice of dispersion proxy --- subject to the realized-volatility caveat above.

The results above summarize performance at 0 bps. The discussion next turns to the portfolio mechanics behind these outcomes---weight paths and turnover---because these will be the primary channel through which proportional transaction costs affect net performance (Section~5).

